  "prompt": "[system]\n\n    You generate compact, robust Python TSP metaheuristics.\n    You must follow the requested interface exactly, always preserve tour validity.\n    \n\n[user]\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000003: farthest_insertion_2opt\n\nStatus: valid; distance: 8.23956\n\nThought: Construct tour using farthest-insertion heuristic: start with farthest pair, then iteratively insert the farthest unvisited city at the position causing minimal distance increase. Then improve with 2-opt local search until no improvement. Report best tour at construction best and after each improvement step.\n\nHistory source: yes\nHistory bucket: tsp:size:100\nHistory role: regime specialist\n\nCode:\n\n```python\n\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    n = len(distance_matrix)\n    if n == 1:\n        return np.array([0])\n    # farthest insertion construction\n    # start with farthest pair\n    start, end = np.unravel_index(np.argmax(distance_matrix), (n, n))\n    tour = [start, end]\n    unvisited = set(range(n)) - {start, end}\n    while unvisited:\n        # find farthest city from tour\n        farthest_city = None\n        farthest_dist = -1\n        for city in unvisited:\n            min_dist = min(distance_matrix[city][t] for t in tour)\n            if min_dist > farthest_dist:\n                farthest_dist = min_dist\n                farthest_city = city\n        # insert farthest_city at best position\n        best_pos = 0\n        best_increase = float('inf')\n        for i in range(len(tour)):\n            j = (i + 1) % len(tour)\n            increase = distance_matrix[tour[i]][farthest_city] + distance_matrix[farthest_city][tour[j]] - distance_matrix[tour[i]][tour[j]]\n            if increase < best_increase:\n                best_increase = increase\n                best_pos = i + 1\n        tour.insert(best_pos, farthest_city)\n        unvisited.remove(farthest_city)\n    tour_arr = np.array(tour)\n    report_best_tour(tour_arr)\n    # 2-opt improvement\n    improved = True\n    while improved:\n        improved = False\n        for i in range(n-2):\n            for k in range(i+2, n):\n                if k == n-1 and i == 0:\n                    continue  # skip wrap-around? Actually handle by reversing segment\n                new_tour = tour_arr.copy()\n                new_tour[i+1:k+1] = tour_arr[i+1:k+1][::-1]\n                # compute delta\n                a, b = tour_arr[i], tour_arr[i+1]\n                c, d = tour_arr[k], tour_arr[(k+1)%n]\n                delta = distance_matrix[a][new_tour[i+1]] + distance_matrix[new_tour[k]][d] - distance_matrix[a][b] - distance_matrix[c][d]\n                if delta < -1e-10:\n                    tour_arr = new_tour\n                    improved = True\n                    report_best_tour(tour_arr)\n    return tour_arr\n\n```\n\nVERBAL GRADIENT: follow the reflection below as the primary source of change.\n\nUse the supplied parents as supporting evidence only.\n\nPARENT-SPECIFIC LLM REFLECTIONS:\n\nParent cand_000003 LLM reflection:\n- Summary: Candidate achieved low mean tour length (8.24) with perfect validity and no timeouts across 30 instances of size 100, using farthest-insertion construction and 2-opt local search.\n- Aim: Further reduce mean tour length by enhancing the local search or adding a diversification mechanism, while maintaining zero timeouts and valid permutations.\n- Preserve: Farthest-insertion construction; 2-opt improvement; report_best_tour usage\n- Change: Introduce a restart mechanism (e.g., random perturbation) if 2-opt stalls; Add an additional local search like Or-opt\n- Avoid: Breaking permutation validity; Increasing runtime leading to timeout; Removing report_best_tour calls; Making random changes without structure\n\nDistance is the search objective for TSP; lower distance is better.\n\nImplement a complete TSP metaheuristic inside this required entrypoint:\n\ndef solve_tsp(distance_matrix: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n\nRequirements:\n- Minimize total TSP tour distance.\n- Return a 1D np.ndarray containing each node exactly once.\n- Do not repeat the start node at the end.\n- Always return a valid tour.\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates.\n- Create a valid tour early.\n- Call report_best_tour(tour) whenever a new best valid tour is found, assume this func already exists.\n- Do not use files, network, subprocesses, or external solvers.\n- Keep the code compact and robust.\n\ncorrect signature, valid tour on every return path,\nearly report_best_tour, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short_snake_case_name\",\n  \"thought\": \"high-level summary of the construction, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_tsp.",
